1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,814
A bus goes all around the
city and back to its depot.

2
00:00:03,814 --> 00:00:06,398
A runner finishes four laps of the track.

3
00:00:06,798 --> 00:00:08,507
Both travel a long way.

4
00:00:08,507 --> 00:00:11,402
And both end up exactly
where they started.

5
00:00:11,852 --> 00:00:16,425
Aarav rides his bicycle to the shop,
one kilometre away, and comes straight

6
00:00:16,425 --> 00:00:17,325
back home.

7
00:00:17,607 --> 00:00:19,884
His trip meter says two kilometres.

8
00:00:19,884 --> 00:00:22,663
But his physics teacher
says he moved zero.

9
00:00:22,663 --> 00:00:23,706
Who is right?

10
00:00:24,206 --> 00:00:25,211
Both of them.

11
00:00:25,211 --> 00:00:27,306
They measure two different things.

12
00:00:27,306 --> 00:00:28,405
Let's see which.

13
00:00:29,055 --> 00:00:30,862
The first one is distance.

14
00:00:30,862 --> 00:00:34,282
Distance is the length of
the actual path you travel.

15
00:00:34,632 --> 00:00:36,850
Every bend and every turn counts.

16
00:00:36,850 --> 00:00:39,943
It only has a size, so
distance is a scalar.

17
00:00:40,393 --> 00:00:42,182
The second one is displacement.

18
00:00:42,182 --> 00:00:45,743
It is the straight arrow from
where you start to where you end.

19
00:00:46,093 --> 00:00:49,769
It has a size and a direction,
so displacement is a vector.

20
00:00:49,769 --> 00:00:52,128
It does not care which path you took.

21
00:00:52,778 --> 00:00:55,425
Let's watch one ride on a straight road.

22
00:00:55,425 --> 00:00:56,698
Home is at zero.

23
00:00:57,048 --> 00:01:01,621
This time Aarav rides forward to the
library, six hundred metres away.

24
00:01:02,021 --> 00:01:06,146
Up to here, distance and displacement
are both six hundred metres.

25
00:01:06,146 --> 00:01:07,466
They grow together.

26
00:01:07,866 --> 00:01:11,883
Now he turns back and rides four
hundred metres to his friend's house.

27
00:01:12,284 --> 00:01:13,530
Watch the graph.

28
00:01:13,530 --> 00:01:15,181
Distance keeps climbing.

29
00:01:15,181 --> 00:01:17,096
Displacement starts to fall.

30
00:01:17,596 --> 00:01:20,034
Let's replay the turn in slow motion.

31
00:01:20,434 --> 00:01:23,157
The wheels keep rolling,
so the path adds up.

32
00:01:23,157 --> 00:01:25,343
But the arrow from home gets shorter.

33
00:01:25,993 --> 00:01:27,056
Final count.

34
00:01:27,056 --> 00:01:29,414
Distance is one thousand metres.

35
00:01:29,414 --> 00:01:32,604
Displacement is two
hundred metres, forward.

36
00:01:33,254 --> 00:01:36,079
Let's write this as a
rule, one step at a time.

37
00:01:36,429 --> 00:01:37,329
Step one.

38
00:01:37,270 --> 00:01:40,716
Distance s is the sum of
every piece of the path.

39
00:01:40,716 --> 00:01:42,818
Six hundred plus four hundred.

40
00:01:42,818 --> 00:01:44,284
One thousand metres.

41
00:01:45,034 --> 00:01:45,934
Step two.

42
00:01:45,914 --> 00:01:49,584
Displacement is final position
minus starting position.

43
00:01:49,584 --> 00:01:52,718
Two hundred minus zero,
two hundred metres.

44
00:01:53,468 --> 00:01:54,368
Step three.

45
00:01:54,355 --> 00:01:58,374
So the size of the displacement
is never more than the distance.

46
00:01:59,124 --> 00:02:00,214
Remember this.

47
00:02:00,214 --> 00:02:02,148
Distance is the whole path.

48
00:02:02,148 --> 00:02:04,642
Displacement is the straight shortcut.

49
00:02:05,342 --> 00:02:07,715
But why can distance never be smaller?

50
00:02:07,715 --> 00:02:10,273
Let's follow the reasoning, link by link.

51
00:02:10,623 --> 00:02:11,523
Link one.

52
00:02:11,406 --> 00:02:15,035
Between two points, a straight
line is the shortest path.

53
00:02:15,435 --> 00:02:16,335
Link two.

54
00:02:16,261 --> 00:02:20,342
Displacement is exactly that
straight line, from start to end.

55
00:02:20,742 --> 00:02:21,700
Link three.

56
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:26,466
Any real path is that line, or
something longer with bends and turns.

57
00:02:26,866 --> 00:02:30,405
So distance is always at least
the size of displacement.

58
00:02:30,405 --> 00:02:33,701
Equal only on a straight
path with no turning back.

59
00:02:34,301 --> 00:02:35,413
Side by side.

60
00:02:35,413 --> 00:02:36,998
Distance is a scalar.

61
00:02:36,998 --> 00:02:40,230
It is never negative,
and it never goes down.

62
00:02:40,630 --> 00:02:42,377
Displacement is a vector.

63
00:02:42,377 --> 00:02:45,158
It can be positive, negative, or zero.

64
00:02:45,158 --> 00:02:47,537
Only the start and the end matter.

65
00:02:48,137 --> 00:02:49,244
Now you try.

66
00:02:49,244 --> 00:02:53,671
Aarav cycles once around a round
park and stops where he began.

67
00:02:53,671 --> 00:02:55,540
Is his displacement zero?

68
00:02:55,540 --> 00:02:56,916
Pause and decide.

69
00:03:00,116 --> 00:03:01,040
Yes, zero!

70
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:02,756
He ends where he started.

71
00:03:02,756 --> 00:03:05,949
But his distance is the
whole way around the park.

72
00:03:06,599 --> 00:03:08,335
Let's test this on a circle.

73
00:03:08,685 --> 00:03:11,720
A dot moves around a circle of radius r.

74
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:13,439
Violet arc: distance.

75
00:03:13,439 --> 00:03:15,375
Blue arrow: displacement.

76
00:03:15,775 --> 00:03:19,250
After a quarter turn, the
distance is pi r by two.

77
00:03:19,250 --> 00:03:21,745
The displacement is root two times r.

78
00:03:22,145 --> 00:03:24,849
After half a turn, distance is pi r.

79
00:03:24,849 --> 00:03:28,848
Displacement is two r, straight
across, its biggest value.

80
00:03:29,248 --> 00:03:32,361
After a full turn, the
distance is two pi r.

81
00:03:32,361 --> 00:03:34,742
The displacement drops back to zero.

82
00:03:35,392 --> 00:03:36,292
Your turn.

83
00:03:36,246 --> 00:03:41,255
On any path, can the distance ever be
less than the size of the displacement?

84
00:03:41,255 --> 00:03:42,512
Pause and decide.

85
00:03:45,712 --> 00:03:46,612
Never!

86
00:03:46,251 --> 00:03:50,472
The displacement is the straight line,
and nothing is shorter than a straight

87
00:03:50,472 --> 00:03:51,372
line.

88
00:03:51,617 --> 00:03:53,353
Now, one worked example.

89
00:03:53,703 --> 00:03:58,237
Aarav cycles half way around a
round park of radius seventy metres.

90
00:03:58,237 --> 00:04:00,924
Find the distance and the displacement.

91
00:04:01,424 --> 00:04:03,962
Distance is half the circumference.

92
00:04:03,962 --> 00:04:05,506
So s equals pi r.

93
00:04:06,256 --> 00:04:11,222
With pi as twenty two by seven, s is
twenty two by seven times seventy.

94
00:04:11,222 --> 00:04:13,343
Two hundred and twenty metres.

95
00:04:14,093 --> 00:04:18,199
Displacement is the straight line
across the park, the diameter.

96
00:04:18,199 --> 00:04:19,683
So it equals two r.

97
00:04:20,433 --> 00:04:21,870
Two times seventy.

98
00:04:21,870 --> 00:04:24,031
One hundred and forty metres.

99
00:04:24,681 --> 00:04:26,146
Now, the exam trap.

100
00:04:26,496 --> 00:04:30,819
Many students say that after one full
round, the displacement is two pi

101
00:04:30,819 --> 00:04:31,719
r.

102
00:04:31,564 --> 00:04:33,537
But two pi r is the distance.

103
00:04:33,537 --> 00:04:37,716
After one full round you are back
at the start, so the displacement is

104
00:04:37,716 --> 00:04:38,616
zero.

105
00:04:38,887 --> 00:04:40,352
Where is this asked?

106
00:04:40,352 --> 00:04:44,728
Short questions on round trips and
circles: distance, displacement, or

107
00:04:44,728 --> 00:04:45,682
their ratio.

108
00:04:46,282 --> 00:04:47,542
Exam level now.

109
00:04:47,542 --> 00:04:52,447
A particle goes three quarters of
the way around a circle of radius R.

110
00:04:52,447 --> 00:04:55,513
Find distance over the
size of displacement.

111
00:04:55,513 --> 00:04:56,828
Pause and solve.

112
00:05:02,228 --> 00:05:03,553
The answer is A.

113
00:05:03,553 --> 00:05:05,721
Three pi, over two root two.

114
00:05:06,221 --> 00:05:09,057
Distance is three quarters of two pi R.

115
00:05:09,057 --> 00:05:11,141
That is three pi R by two.

116
00:05:11,641 --> 00:05:15,985
Start and end are ninety degrees
apart, so the displacement is root two

117
00:05:15,985 --> 00:05:16,885
R.

118
00:05:16,882 --> 00:05:17,782
Divide.

119
00:05:17,583 --> 00:05:22,740
Three pi R by two, over root two R,
gives three pi over two root two.

120
00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:24,533
About three point three.

121
00:05:25,133 --> 00:05:26,590
Here is your recap.

122
00:05:26,590 --> 00:05:28,088
Screenshot this card.

123
00:05:31,288 --> 00:05:34,125
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

124
00:05:34,525 --> 00:05:35,425
One.

125
00:05:35,006 --> 00:05:38,402
Can the distance be zero
while the displacement is not?

126
00:05:39,302 --> 00:05:40,202
Two.

127
00:05:39,848 --> 00:05:43,186
When are distance and
displacement exactly equal?

128
00:05:44,086 --> 00:05:44,986
Three.

129
00:05:44,725 --> 00:05:49,324
What is the displacement after
half a lap of a circle of radius r?

130
00:05:50,424 --> 00:05:54,347
Next time: two cyclists, same
speed, opposite directions.

131
00:05:54,347 --> 00:05:56,773
Same speed, but different velocity?

132
00:05:56,773 --> 00:05:58,694
That is speed and velocity.
